% What a dialect inherits from `ansi` when it overrides nothing.
%
% `ansi` is `target: false` -- you cannot translate to it, and the refusal says
% so. Every shipped target either IS mysql-family, postgresql or sqlite or
% descends through one, and each of those overrides most of what ansi declares.
% Six ansi entries are therefore shadowed by every target, which meant no case,
% no fuzz lane and no oracle had ever evaluated them.
%
% They are not dead. `Map::defineDialect(name, ['extends' => 'ansi'])` is the
% documented way to add a server, and it resolves exactly these. A user reaching
% for it -- as the first external user of this layer did on their first day,
% though they extended mariadb -- gets the portable spelling, unexercised.
%
% Two mechanisms could have caught this and neither can:
%
%   - `caveat_pins` in php/bin/sqlt requires a string pin for every caveated
%     entry, but it walks `Sql::dialects()`, which is the shipped TARGETS. Four
%     of the six carry caveats -- trim-charset on the TRIM family, concat-null
%     on `&` -- and none was ever demanded.
%   - The semantic oracle needs a DSN per dialect, and there is no ANSI server:
%     ANSI is a standard, not something anyone ships. See the README note below
%     on what that leaves unverified.
%
% So these are STRING pins and only string pins. They say "this is the SQL a
% bare ansi descendant emits", which is the half that can be checked without a
% server, and it is the half that was missing entirely. What they do NOT say is
% that the SQL means what SEL means -- for the four caveated entries the map
% already declares it does not.
%
% The registration is repeated per case because the runner calls Map::reset()
% before each one. A registration case is exempt from the mirror, so nothing
% here is re-run under another dialect.

### name: ansi.trim.strips-only-what-the-standard-strips
--- note
The caveat this file exists for. SEL's TRIM strips space, tab, CR and LF;
`TRIM(BOTH FROM x)` strips the pad character, which is a space. Every shipped
target overrides this with something that names the four characters -- a
REGEXP_REPLACE on the MySQL family, btrim with an explicit set on PostgreSQL --
so `trim-charset` has never fired anywhere. It fires here.
--- dialect
ansi-probe
--- register
[{"dialect": "ansi-probe", "extends": "ansi", "version": "1", "target": true}]
--- source
TRIM(" a ")
--- expect
TRIM(BOTH FROM CAST(' a ' AS CHARACTER VARYING))
===
### name: ansi.ltrim.leading-only
--- dialect
ansi-probe
--- register
[{"dialect": "ansi-probe", "extends": "ansi", "version": "1", "target": true}]
--- source
LTRIM(" a")
--- expect
TRIM(LEADING FROM CAST(' a' AS CHARACTER VARYING))
===
### name: ansi.rtrim.trailing-only
--- dialect
ansi-probe
--- register
[{"dialect": "ansi-probe", "extends": "ansi", "version": "1", "target": true}]
--- source
RTRIM("a ")
--- expect
TRIM(TRAILING FROM CAST('a ' AS CHARACTER VARYING))
===
### name: ansi.substr.two-arguments
--- note
The only uncaveated ansi function template, and it is by-count: two arguments
take FROM alone, three add FOR. Both arms are pinned, because a by-count entry
whose arms disagree is a defect no single case can see.

The casts are not decoration. SEL numbers ARE text, so SUBSTR's subject may be a
number and its positions may be text; MySQL coerces both silently and a
conformant server does not -- `SUBSTRING('abc' FROM 1.0)` is
`function substring(unknown, numeric) does not exist` on PostgreSQL 17. Standard
SQL is strongly typed, so the standard spelling says so.
--- dialect
ansi-probe
--- register
[{"dialect": "ansi-probe", "extends": "ansi", "version": "1", "target": true}]
--- source
SUBSTR("abcd", 2)
--- expect
SUBSTRING(CAST('abcd' AS CHARACTER VARYING) FROM CAST(2 AS INTEGER))
===
### name: ansi.substr.three-arguments
--- dialect
ansi-probe
--- register
[{"dialect": "ansi-probe", "extends": "ansi", "version": "1", "target": true}]
--- source
SUBSTR("abcd", 2, 2)
--- expect
SUBSTRING(CAST('abcd' AS CHARACTER VARYING) FROM CAST(2 AS INTEGER) FOR CAST(2 AS INTEGER))
===
### name: ansi.multiply
--- note
The casts are the standard being strongly typed. SEL numbers are text, so both
operands may arrive as text literals, and `'10' / '4'` is
`operator is not unique: unknown / unknown` on PostgreSQL 17 -- ambiguous
rather than wrong, which is worse, because one untyped operand resolves and two
do not. MariaDB coerces either way and keeps the bare spelling in
mysql-family.json, where a reader meets both.

`NUMERIC` carries no precision on purpose. Standard SQL leaves it
implementation-defined, and SEL's numbers have no fixed width, so any
`DECIMAL(p,s)` here is a ceiling somebody eventually meets: ansi said
`DECIMAL(38,10)` until the oracle ran a 38-digit literal through it and
PostgreSQL answered `22003 numeric field overflow`, and a scale of 10 had been
quietly truncating anything smaller. PostgreSQL and SQLite already spell it
this way; MariaDB needs its own ceiling and states `DECIMAL(65,10)`.
--- dialect
ansi-probe
--- register
[{"dialect": "ansi-probe", "extends": "ansi", "version": "1", "target": true}]
--- source
2 * 3
--- expect
(CAST(2 AS NUMERIC) * CAST(3 AS NUMERIC))
===
### name: ansi.concat.double-pipe
--- note
`||` is the standard concatenation operator and carries `concat-null`: in
standard SQL a NULL operand makes the whole result NULL, where SEL's `&` over a
NONE raises E_NO_SCALAR rather than yielding one. The MySQL family overrides
this with CONCAT for its own reasons; nothing has ever emitted this spelling.
--- dialect
ansi-probe
--- register
[{"dialect": "ansi-probe", "extends": "ansi", "version": "1", "target": true}]
--- source
"a" & "b"
--- expect
(CAST('a' AS CHARACTER VARYING) || CAST('b' AS CHARACTER VARYING))
===
### name: ansi.identifiers-are-double-quoted
--- note
Not an inherited entry but the lexical vocabulary underneath them, which is
what makes the six above readable on a standard server and unreadable on
MySQL without ANSI_QUOTES. Worth one case: the pins above would all still pass
if identQuote drifted, since none of them names a column.
--- dialect
ansi-probe
--- register
[{"dialect": "ansi-probe", "extends": "ansi", "version": "1", "target": true}]
--- bindings
{"N": {"kind": "column", "column": "n", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
N == 25
--- expect
("n" = 25)
===
### name: ansi.no-regex-so-no-mapping
--- note
Twenty-six of ansi's entries are refusals rather than templates -- a reason
string, not SQL -- and they are unreachable for the same reason the six above
were. They are pinned by code and not by message: spec/errors.md says codes are
contract and messages are not, so twenty-six message pins would be pinning
something the layer does not promise. This one stands for the shape.
--- dialect
ansi-probe
--- register
[{"dialect": "ansi-probe", "extends": "ansi", "version": "1", "target": true}]
--- source
RMATCH("^a", "abc")
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED 1:1
===
### name: ansi.inherits-isnum-and-still-cannot-guard
--- note
The kind warrant, met by a dialect nobody wrote for. ansi refuses ISNUM -- no
regex, and no failure-tolerant cast -- so it declares no numericGuard either,
and an operand nobody vouched for cannot be read as a number. Extending ansi
bare therefore gets sqlite's treatment and not the MySQL family's, which is the
right answer and was nowhere written down.

Declaring the binding NUM translates it again; see the identifier case above,
which does exactly that.
--- dialect
ansi-probe
--- register
[{"dialect": "ansi-probe", "extends": "ansi", "version": "1", "target": true}]
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
T == 25
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED 1:1
===
